---
scope: src/tools/brain
description: >
  Runtime brain entry-point tool — when an agent reaches for the brain mid-task,
  takes an optional `kind` discriminator (`"orientation" | "config"`, default
  `"orientation"`) and returns the verbatim bytes of the matching named section
  from the host's `.aide/config/brain.aide` alongside a status, with no
  server-side substitution, no registry dispatch, and a response shape that
  carries only `status` + `instructions` (no `backend`, no `connector`, no
  `name`).
intent: >
  aide_brain is the runtime brain entry-point. When an agent reaches for the brain
  mid-task, it calls this tool with an optional `kind` parameter and receives
  either the verbatim bytes of the matching named body section the user hand-wrote
  in `.aide/config/brain.aide` (on the `ok` branch) or a short, backend-agnostic
  remediation sentence telling the user exactly which command to run to recover
  (on every non-ok branch). The tool exposes exactly two live sections to agents
  through `kind` — `orientation` (the default; the runtime briefing returned when
  an agent reaches for the brain mid-task) and `config` (the integration-specific
  wiring flow, used by `/aide:brain config`) — and reads no other body section.
  The remaining sections of `brain.aide` (`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
  `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`) are install-time seeds owned by the install
  service, not live reads, and this tool does not surface them under any `kind`.
  The tool never composes prose from package-side templates, never dispatches
  through a backend registry, and never runs any substitution pass over the
  selected section — what the user wrote is what the agent reads, byte-identical.
  The server's only runtime job is to resolve the brain precondition state via
  `buildBrainState`, dispatch on `kind` to pick the section, and either surface
  that section's bytes or name the recovery action. Once the agent reads the
  section, the brain itself takes over; this tool has no further role in
  navigation.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - "The response shape is exactly `{ status, instructions }` — no `backend`
       field, no `connector` field, no `name` field, no other discriminant. The
       user's `name` label lives inside `brain.aide` for the human reader; it is
       NOT threaded into the agent-facing tool result. Consumers branch on
       `status` alone."
    - "The tool accepts a single optional argument `kind` whose vocabulary is
       exactly `\"orientation\" | \"config\"`. When omitted, `kind` defaults to
       `\"orientation\"` — that default produces the same response shape and
       bytes as the previous parameter-less contract did for what was then the
       `prose` section. Any value outside `\"orientation\" | \"config\"` is an
       input-validation failure surfaced by the MCP framework before the handler
       runs; the handler never branches on a third or unknown value."
    - "On `status: ok`, `instructions` contains the verbatim bytes of exactly
       one named body section from `.aide/config/brain.aide`, selected by `kind`:
       `kind: \"orientation\"` returns `parseBrainAide`'s `orientation` field,
       and `kind: \"config\"` returns `parseBrainAide`'s `config` field. Each
       field is the bytes the parser already extracted from between the matching
       marker pair, byte-identical to what the user wrote. No trimming, no
       normalization, no template rendering, no `${...}` substitution of any
       kind. This tool does NOT itself parse markers, walk headings, or otherwise
       re-extract any section region — `parseBrainAide` owns that extraction and
       this tool consumes its output."
    - "The set of body sections this tool surfaces to agents is exactly
       `orientation` and `config` — the two live agent-facing sections. The
       install-time seed sections `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
       `updatePlaybook`, and `researchIndex` are owned by the install service
       and are never returned by this tool under any `kind`. Per-consumer
       ownership: `aide_brain` reads `orientation` and `config`; the install
       service reads the seed sections. Adding a new live section to the
       `kind` vocabulary is a parent-side decision that requires a coordinated
       change to `BrainAideConfig`, this tool's `kind` schema, and this spec —
       it is not implicit."
    - "On `status: no-brain-aide`, `instructions` is a fixed remediation sentence
       directing the user to run `npx aidemd-mcp init` to scaffold the brain
       config file at `.aide/config/brain.aide`. The agent surfaces this and
       stops; it does not proceed as if the brain were available. The remediation
       prose is identical regardless of which `kind` was requested — non-ok
       branches do not vary by `kind` because the file the section would be
       drawn from is unavailable in the same way for every section."
