       different clothing."
---

## Context

The user-owned `.aide/config/brain.aide` body carries six marker-bounded sections,
and those sections split into two categories by *who reads them*. The first two —
`orientation` and `config` — are **live** sections: they live in `brain.aide`
permanently and are read on demand at runtime. This tool is the only path agents
have to reach them. The other four — `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
`updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex` — are **install-time seeds**: the install service
reads them exactly once on cold init and writes their bytes into the brain as
on-disk entry-point artifacts (`coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`,
`coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`, `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`,
`research/research.md`). After install the brain owns those files; agents read
them through the brain's read tool, not through `aide_brain`. The seed sections in
`brain.aide` go dormant — they only matter again if the user wipes their brain and
re-inits.

This tool is the **live-section tool**. Its `kind` parameter selects which of the
two live sections to return. `kind: "orientation"` (the default) returns the
runtime briefing — what the brain is, which MCP tools call to read it, where the
four entry-point artifacts live, how to walk them. Agents call this whenever they
reach for the brain mid-task. `kind: "config"` returns the integration-specific
wiring flow — the prose that `/aide:brain config` executes verbatim to walk a user
through landing their brain's identifier (a path, an API token, a JSON blob,
whatever the integration needs) into `mcpServerConfig.args` and running sync. The
`config` section *cannot* live in the brain itself because it runs *before* the
brain is wired — there is no brain to read it from.

`update-playbook` deliberately is **not** a `kind` value. The maintenance
methodology it carries seeds `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md` in the brain at
install time; after install, the user edits that file in the brain. The shipped
`/aide:update-playbook` command is a thin pointer at the on-disk artifact, exactly
the shape the existing `study-playbook` skill follows. Letting `aide_brain` return
the seed bytes would create a second authoritative source for the same content
(the `brain.aide` section AND the on-disk artifact) and lie to the agent about
which one its edits should target. The same reasoning extends to every other seed
section: `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `researchIndex` all become brain files
once installed; agents read them through the brain.

The response shape is `{ status, instructions }` and nothing else. The pre-rework
contract carried `backend: <id>` as a discriminant consumers branched on
programmatically; that field is retired along with the in-code backend registry it
served. Adding `name`, `connector`, or any per-kind discriminant on the wire
re-creates that registry-dispatch temptation in different clothing — just on the
consumer side instead of the server side. Branching on `status` alone is the entire
control surface this tool exposes.

This tool is on-demand only — called by agents that need the brain mid-task and by
`/aide:brain config` during bootstrap, never fired on every `/aide` boot. The boot
probe (`aide_info`) already reports brain precondition state at boot time using the
same `BrainState` vocabulary. Firing this tool at boot would duplicate that work
and bloat every session with prose the agent throws away if the task does not need
the brain.

## Strategy

**Two `kind` values, not three, not six.** The tool's vocabulary mirrors the
live-section pair exactly: `orientation` and `config`. `update-playbook` is the
near-miss case — it is a sibling of `study-playbook` in `brain.aide`, both are
methodology documents, and a naive read of "they look similar, expose them
similarly" would add it as a third `kind`. The reason it is rejected: after install,
the live source for the maintenance methodology is the brain file
(`coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`), not the `brain.aide` seed bytes. Returning
the seed through `aide_brain` would let an agent edit the `brain.aide` section and
expect those edits to reach the playbook the agent uses next session — but next
session's `/aide:update-playbook` reads the brain file, which the seed-side edit
never touched. The same regression class applies to every other install-time seed
(`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `researchIndex`). The two-kind vocabulary is
load-bearing: it is the schema-level enforcement of the live-vs-seed split.

**Schema-level rejection of unknown `kind` values, not handler-level.** The MCP
framework validates the registered input schema before the handler runs. A `kind`
outside `"orientation" | "config"` fails at the framework boundary with a
structured input-validation error; the handler never sees it. The handler
exhaustively switches on the two sanctioned values and returns from one of those
branches — no default branch, no `throw new Error("unknown kind")`, no fallback to
`orientation` for an unrecognized input. Schema-level rejection is the only shape
that gets the live-vs-seed enforcement right by construction: a future contributor
who adds `"update-playbook"` to the `kind` schema cannot ship that change without
this spec changing too, because the handler's exhaustive switch will fail to
type-check until the new branch is wired. Handler-level fallbacks would let a
typo or a misunderstanding silently downgrade to `orientation` and look correct in
manual testing.

**Response shape stays at `{ status, instructions }` regardless of `kind`.** The
pre-rework registry returned `backend: <id>` so consumers could vary behavior per
connector — exactly the dispatch pattern the refactor retired. Re-introducing any
discriminant alongside `status` and `instructions` (a `name` field reflecting the
user's brain label, a `kind` field echoing what was requested, a `section` field
naming what was returned) re-creates that pattern on the consumer side. The
defense is structural: the wire shape carries no discriminant the calling agent
could branch on except `status`. The user's `name` label lives inside `brain.aide`
for the human reading the file and for the agent narrating in conversation; it
never crosses the tool boundary. The `kind` argument is the consumer's input, not
the consumer's output — echoing it back invites consumers to treat it as a
dispatch axis the next release will be tempted to honor.

**Non-ok branches return identical remediation prose regardless of `kind`.** The
three non-ok states (`no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`) describe
conditions where the file the section would be drawn from is unavailable in the
same way for every section. There is no `kind`-specific recovery — running
`npx aidemd-mcp init` scaffolds `brain.aide` once, after which both `orientation`
and `config` become readable; running `npx aidemd-mcp sync` fixes drift once,
regardless of which section the agent was reaching for. Varying the remediation
prose by `kind` would imply the user has a different recovery path for each
section, which is false and would mislead the agent. The remediation strings are
fixed top-of-file constants — testable verbatim, immune to per-call composition,
naming the exact terminal command the user must type.

**One `parseBrainAide` call per `ok` invocation; section selection is in-memory
dispatch.** The `ok` branch reads `brain.aide` exactly once via `parseBrainAide`,
then dispatches on `kind` against the parser's already-extracted named fields —
returning `result.orientation` for `kind: "orientation"` and `result.config` for
`kind: "config"`. The tool does not re-parse the file per `kind`, does not parse
markers itself, and does not cache parsed results across calls in a module-scope
variable. Per-call read keeps the tool faithful to the user's most recent edit
without coupling to file-watcher infrastructure; in-memory dispatch keeps the read
cost flat regardless of `kind`. The pre-rework codebase had a single-section
contract and a matching single-field read; this rework preserves that exactly,
except now the parsed result has six named fields and the tool picks one.

**`instructions` is non-empty on every branch, including the defensive fallback.**
On `ok`, `instructions` is the verbatim section bytes — non-empty by construction
because `parseBrainAide` rejects empty marker pairs as `malformed-body`. On every
non-ok branch, `instructions` is one of the fixed remediation sentences. The
defensive fallback covers the narrow window where `buildBrainState` returns `ok`
but `parseBrainAide` returns a non-ok kind (a race between file deletion and the
two calls, or a future refactor that relaxes the detector's file check); that
fallback routes to the `no-brain-aide` remediation prose because the
upstream-contract-violation pattern this guard exists for is "detector says ok but
parser disagrees" — a missing-or-broken-file shape. A caller can unconditionally
surface `instructions` to the agent and get an actionable response.

**Status vocabulary mirrors `BrainState` exactly.** The four statuses (`ok`,
`no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`) flow from `buildBrainState`
unchanged; this tool adds nothing and removes nothing. The retired `invalid-path`
state goes away because `rootPath` was removed from the schema — the package no
longer validates a brain location, so there is no path-validation status to
surface. This spec deliberately does not pin a count of states or enumerate the
membership inline; `BrainState` is the single source of truth for which statuses
exist. Adding a state is a parent-side change that flows through automatically.

## Good examples

A project with a properly configured `brain.aide` and matching `.mcp.json` entry.
The user's `orientation` section (the bytes between
`<!-- aide-orientation-start -->` and `<!-- aide-orientation-end -->`) reads:

```
Your brain is an external knowledge store reached through MCP tools.
Use `mcp__brain__read_note` to open files by their brain-relative path.
Use `mcp__brain__search_notes` for keyword queries across every file in
the store. The store has four entry-point artifacts: the coding-playbook
index at `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`, the study-playbook
navigation guide at `coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`, the
update-playbook maintenance guide at `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`,
and the research index at `research/research.md`. Start from the
relevant entry-point for your task, follow the references it lists to
deepen context, and check those files' references too. Stay in scope;
don't follow references into unrelated topics.
```

An agent calls `aide_brain` with no arguments — `kind` defaults to `"orientation"`:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "instructions": "Your brain is an external knowledge store reached through MCP tools.\nUse `mcp__brain__read_note` to open files by their brain-relative path.\nUse `mcp__brain__search_notes` for keyword queries across every file in\nthe store. The store has four entry-point artifacts: the coding-playbook\nindex at `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`, the study-playbook\nnavigation guide at `coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`, the\nupdate-playbook maintenance guide at `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`,\nand the research index at `research/research.md`. Start from the\nrelevant entry-point for your task, follow the references it lists to\ndeepen context, and check those files' references too. Stay in scope;\ndon't follow references into unrelated topics."
}
```

No `backend` field, no `connector`, no `name`, no echoed `kind`. The
`instructions` text is the user's `orientation` section verbatim — sourced from
`parseBrainAide`'s `orientation` field on the parsed result, with no
server-composed sentences and no template substitution.

The same project's `config` section (the bytes between `<!-- aide-config-start -->`
and `<!-- aide-config-end -->`) reads:

```
You are completing the wiring of an Obsidian brain. The required value is the
absolute path to the user's Obsidian vault, landed as the last entry of
`mcpServerConfig.args` in `brain.aide`.

Argument shape (Obsidian only): `/aide:brain config [<absolute-path>]`. When
`$ARGUMENTS` is non-empty, treat it as the absolute path the user wants to wire
(initial wiring) or re-wire to (re-point). Empty `$ARGUMENTS` means "ask
interactively" on a fresh wire and "STOP, nothing to do" against an already-wired
brain.

1. Read `brain.aide`. Extract the current path entry from `mcpServerConfig.args`
   (a YAML null at `args[3]` — a bare `-` with no scalar after it — means
   un-wired; any string at that index means already wired).
2. Decide the target path:
   - `$ARGUMENTS` non-empty → use it as the target path.
   - `$ARGUMENTS` empty AND `args[3]` is null → ask the user where their vault
     lives. Use `AskUserQuestion` with `aide_info.brain.hints` as suggestions
     plus a "Different location" entry.
   - `$ARGUMENTS` empty AND `args[3]` is a real path → STOP, nothing to do.
3. Edit `brain.aide` — replace the current `args[3]` entry with the target path.
4. Sync — read `.mcp.json["mcpServers"]["aide"]`, take its command and args, append
   `"sync"`, run via Bash. On exit 0 continue; on non-zero surface stderr and stop.
5. Emit the restart message verbatim: "Sync wrote the brain entry. Restart Claude
   Code so the brain MCP server picks up the new entry, then re-run /aide."
```

`/aide:brain config` calls `aide_brain({ kind: "config" })` to pull the wiring
flow as the second step of its router shape. The tool returns:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "instructions": "You are completing the wiring of an Obsidian brain. The required value is the\nabsolute path to the user's Obsidian vault, landed as the last entry of\n`mcpServerConfig.args` in `brain.aide`.\n\nArgument shape (Obsidian only): `/aide:brain config [<absolute-path>]`. When\n`$ARGUMENTS` is non-empty, treat it as the absolute path the user wants to wire\n(initial wiring) or re-wire to (re-point). Empty `$ARGUMENTS` means \"ask\ninteractively\" on a fresh wire and \"STOP, nothing to do\" against an already-wired\nbrain.\n\n1. Read `brain.aide`. Extract the current path entry from `mcpServerConfig.args`\n   (a YAML null at `args[3]` — a bare `-` with no scalar after it — means\n   un-wired; any string at that index means already wired).\n2. Decide the target path:\n   - `$ARGUMENTS` non-empty → use it as the target path.\n   - `$ARGUMENTS` empty AND `args[3]` is null → ask the user where their vault\n     lives. Use `AskUserQuestion` with `aide_info.brain.hints` as suggestions\n     plus a \"Different location\" entry.\n   - `$ARGUMENTS` empty AND `args[3]` is a real path → STOP, nothing to do.\n3. Edit `brain.aide` — replace the current `args[3]` entry with the target path.\n4. Sync — read `.mcp.json[\"mcpServers\"][\"aide\"]`, take its command and args, append\n   `\"sync\"`, run via Bash. On exit 0 continue; on non-zero surface stderr and stop.\n5. Emit the restart message verbatim: \"Sync wrote the brain entry. Restart Claude\n   Code so the brain MCP server picks up the new entry, then re-run /aide.\""
}
```

Same shape — `{ status, instructions }` — same byte-identical extraction from the
parser's named field, just keyed on the other live section. The router executes
the returned prose verbatim; the shipped command knows nothing about `args[3]`,
YAML null, paths, or argument shapes — only this integration's `config` body
section names that slot. A different integration's `config` section might
identify a different arg index as the unwired slot, document a JSON-blob
argument, or describe an API-token-and-page-id pair across two indexes; the
router does not care, because the integration's prose alone interprets
`$ARGUMENTS` and locates the slot to fill.

A project where `brain.aide` is absent — the user has not run `aide_init` yet. An
agent calls `aide_brain` (default `orientation`):

```json
{
  "status": "no-brain-aide",
  "instructions": "No `.aide/config/brain.aide` is present in this project. Tell the user to run `npx aidemd-mcp init` to scaffold the brain config file, or `/aide:brain config` if they want to be walked through it. Do not proceed with this task as if the brain were available — the file must exist before brain-dependent work continues."
}
```

The same project, the same agent, calling `aide_brain({ kind: "config" })`
returns the **identical** body — same status, same `instructions`. Non-ok prose
does not vary by `kind` because the file the section would be drawn from is
unavailable in the same way for every section, and the recovery (run `init`) is
the same for both.

A project where `brain.aide` and `.mcp.json` disagree — the user edited
`brain.aide` and forgot to run sync. Whichever `kind` the agent passes:

```json
{
  "status": "mcp-drift",
  "instructions": "`.aide/config/brain.aide` and `.mcp.json` disagree about the brain entry. Tell the user to run `npx aidemd-mcp sync` in their terminal — that is the only command that mutates `.mcp.json` from `brain.aide`. Then restart Claude Code. Do not attempt to patch `.mcp.json` from this session."
}
```

## Bad examples

A tool that accepts `kind: "update-playbook"` and returns the seed section's
bytes:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "instructions": "# Update Playbook\n\nMaintenance methodology for the coding playbook. Use `mcp__brain__read_note` to read entries..."
}
```

This is the load-bearing regression. The maintenance methodology's authoritative
source after install is `coding-playbook/update-playbook.md` in the brain — that
is the file `/aide:update-playbook` reads, that is the file the user edits. The
`updatePlaybook` section in `brain.aide` is the seed bytes that scaffolded that
file once on cold install; once the brain file exists, the seed section is
dormant. Returning the seed bytes through `aide_brain` creates a second
authoritative source: an agent that calls `aide_brain({ kind: "update-playbook" })`
and edits what it reads is editing the wrong file. The next call to
`/aide:update-playbook` reads the on-disk brain file, which never received the
edit, and the agent's "update" is silently invisible. The schema's live-vs-seed
split is enforceable at the tool surface or it is not enforced at all.

A tool that accepts `kind: "playbookIndex"` (or `"playbook-index"`,
`"studyPlaybook"`, `"study-playbook"`, `"researchIndex"`, `"research-index"`) and
returns the matching seed section:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "instructions": "# Coding Playbook\n\n## Task Routing\n\n| Task domain | Section |\n|-------------|---------|\n..."
}
```

Same regression in different clothing. Every install-time seed section has an
on-disk brain file that became authoritative after install; exposing any of them
through `aide_brain` re-creates the dual-source-of-truth problem. The `kind`
vocabulary is exactly `"orientation" | "config"` — there is no fifth, sixth, or
seventh value, regardless of whether a contributor names it kebab-case or
camelCase.

A handler that accepts `kind` outside the schema's vocabulary and falls back or
throws inside the handler body:

```ts
async function handler({ kind }: { kind?: string }) {
  switch (kind ?? "orientation") {
    case "orientation": return readSection(parsed.orientation);
    case "config":      return readSection(parsed.config);
    default:            return readSection(parsed.orientation); // fallback
  }
}
```

Or:

```ts
async function handler({ kind }: { kind?: string }) {
  if (kind !== "orientation" && kind !== "config") {
    throw new Error(`unknown kind: ${kind}`);
  }
  // ...
}
```

Both shapes accept input the schema should have rejected. The first silently
downgrades to `orientation` for any value the framework let through, masking
schema drift — a contributor who adds `"update-playbook"` to the schema and forgets
to wire the handler branch ships a tool that returns the wrong section without
warning. The second turns a structured input-validation failure into an unstructured
runtime exception, polluting the response surface with errors the framework was
supposed to convert into typed validation responses upstream. The handler trusts
the schema: it exhaustively switches on `"orientation" | "config"` and the type
checker enforces exhaustiveness — no default, no throw.

A handler that re-runs `parseBrainAide` per `kind` instead of selecting
in-memory:

```ts
async function handler({ kind }: { kind: "orientation" | "config" }) {
  const orientationParse = await parseBrainAide(root); // read 1
  if (kind === "orientation") return readField(orientationParse, "orientation");
  const configParse = await parseBrainAide(root);      // read 2 — wrong
  return readField(configParse, "config");
}
```

The parser already extracts every named section on a single read; calling it twice
is wasted I/O, doubles the surface for a between-reads file change to introduce
inconsistency, and signals a confused mental model where the parser is keyed on
something it is not. One `parseBrainAide` call per `ok` invocation; section
selection is in-memory dispatch on the parsed result's named fields.

A response shape that adds a discriminant to `{ status, instructions }`:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "kind": "orientation",
  "instructions": "..."
}
```

Or:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "name": "my-brain",
  "instructions": "..."
}
```

Or:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "backend": "some-connector",
  "instructions": "..."
}
```

Each shape gives consumers a label they can branch on programmatically —
re-introducing connector-dispatch on the caller side even though the server side
removed it. Echoing `kind` back is the subtler version of the same regression: the
caller already knows what they asked for, so the field exists only as a hook for
future per-`kind` consumer logic. The response carries `status` + `instructions`
only; everything else either lives inside `brain.aide` for the human reader or is
not part of this tool's contract.

A tool that runs a substitution pass over the section bytes before returning
them:

```json
{
  "status": "ok",
  "instructions": "Your brain is at D:/brains/my-brain. Use mcp__brain__read_note..."
}
```

Here `${...}` was replaced with `D:/brains/my-brain` in the section before
returning. Violates the verbatim-bytes invariant on either live section. The
user's section may legitimately contain `${...}` sequences documenting a
templating language, or may simply not use substitution at all; the substitution
engine has no authority over agent-facing prose. It belongs only in
`mcpServerConfig.args` at install/sync time.

A tool that re-introduces an `invalid-path` branch:

```ts
if (state.status === "invalid-path") return { status, instructions: INVALID_PATH_PROSE };
```

`invalid-path` is no longer a `BrainState` value — `rootPath` was removed from the
`brain.aide` schema, so the package no longer has a path to validate. A branch
that handles it is dead code; an `INVALID_PATH_PROSE` constant is dead text.
Re-adding either is the schema-collapse regression in different clothing.

## References

- `D:/Code/Me/aidemd-mcp/BRAIN-CONFIG-REWORK-PLAN.md` -- design doc for this
  rework. The "Two patterns, one rule" section established the live-vs-seed
  partitioning of `brain.aide`'s six body sections (live: `orientation`, `config`;
  seeds: `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`) and
  drove the Context paragraph on why `update-playbook` is a brain file rather
  than a third `kind`. The "aide_brain API change" section pinned the
  `kind?: "orientation" | "config"` vocabulary, the `"orientation"` default, and
  the rule that non-ok branches return identical remediation prose regardless of
  `kind` — anchoring the two-kind Strategy decision and the kind-agnostic non-ok
  Strategy decision. The "New `/aide:update-playbook` flow" section pinned the
  thin-pointer pattern for the shipped command, mirroring `study-playbook` —
  source of the Bad example showing why exposing `update-playbook` as a `kind`
  would create a dual-source-of-truth regression. The bundled-template
  orientation and config section drafts in the same plan supplied the concrete
  prose the Good examples render against.
- `.aide/intent.aide` (brain-layer outcomes) -- parent root spec; established
  that `brain.aide` body sections return verbatim with no server-side
  substitution, that the response shape carries `status` + `instructions` only
  (no `backend`, no `connector`, no `name`), that the in-code backend registry
  is retired entirely, that the `BrainState` vocabulary covers the recovery
  surface (`ok`, `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`), and that exposing
  any install-time seed section through `aide_brain` re-creates the
  registry-dispatch regression. Every Strategy decision in this spec traces to
  one of those parent outcomes.
- `.aide/intent.aide` (body-schema outcome) -- parent root spec; established
  the six-section marker grammar (`<!-- aide-orientation-start -->` /
  `<!-- aide-orientation-end -->` and the matching pairs for `config`,
  `playbook-index`, `study-playbook`, `update-playbook`, `research-index`),
  recognized lowercase and case-sensitive in fixed order, and the typed-key
  spelling rule (kebab-case markers populate camelCase fields:
  `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`). Drove
  the Strategy paragraph on in-memory section selection from
  `parseBrainAide`'s named fields and the Bad example listing every seed-section
  spelling that must be rejected.
- `.aide/intent.aide` (storage-agnostic vocabulary outcome) -- parent root spec;
  established the forbidden-token list and the rule that those tokens must not
  appear in any `.aide` spec authored in this repo nor in code identifiers,
  comments, or string constants project-wide. Drove the storage-agnostic
  framing across this body — Context describes the brain layer in
  storage-agnostic terms (`brain`, `brain root`, the named sections), the Good
  examples use connector-neutral prose for the orientation and config sections,
  and the anti-example carve-out scoping confines forbidden tokens to
  undesired-outcome blocks only.
- `src/.aide` -- parent submodule spec; established that the runtime brain
  entry-point tool reads the host's user-owned brain configuration file and
  returns its hand-written agent-facing sections verbatim, that the package no
  longer enumerates connectors or composes per-connector prose from inline
  templates, and that the boot probe reports brain precondition state at boot
  time — anchoring the Context paragraph on this tool's on-demand-only
  invocation contract.
- `src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide` -- sibling parser spec; defines the
  tagged-result contract (`kind: ok | missing | malformed-frontmatter |
  malformed-body`), the typed `ok` variant carrying `orientation`, `config`,
  `playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, and `researchIndex` as
  separately-named fields each verbatim from between its marker pair, the
  never-throws invariant, the two-field frontmatter schema (`name` +
  `mcpServerConfig`), and the `interpolateArgs` partitioning that ensures
  substitution never reaches any of the six body sections. The `ok` branch of
  this tool depends on the verbatim guarantee specifically of the `orientation`
  and `config` fields; this tool does not parse markers itself, it consumes the
  parser's already-extracted slices.
- `src/service/buildBrainState/.aide` -- sibling detector spec; defines the
  `BrainState` tagged union this tool forwards unchanged, the
  no-`backend`-field contract, and the no-registry invariant. The tool's
  branching logic is a direct consumer of this detector's output; the
  defensive fallback for "detector says ok but parser disagrees" routes to
  `no-brain-aide` because that is the missing-or-broken-file recovery the
  detector's `ok` claim would have masked.
